          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview"} Overview
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_a_universal_characteristic_class"} As a universal characteristic class
              %li
                %a{:href => "#literature"} Literature
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            (representing say a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
            \.
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            is a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+spaces"} tangent spaces
            at all points are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+subspaces"} Lagrangian subspaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+subspaces"} isotropic subspaces
            with respect to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
          %p
            The
            %em Maslov index
            is an invariant of a smooth path in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
          %p
            The Maslov index can be reinterpreted as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            of theories of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+cobordism"} Lagrangian and Legendrean cobordism
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#as_a_universal_characteristic_class As a universal characteristic class
          %p
            The first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            of the stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+Grassmannian"} Lagrangian Grassmannian
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            is isomorphic to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi LGrass
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^1(LGrass, \mathbb{Z})
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathbb{Z}
                  \\,.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} The
            generator of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            is called the
            %em universal Maslov index
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi LGrass
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  u \in H^1(LGrass, \mathbb{Z})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LGrass
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LGrass
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundles"} tangent bundles
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifolds"} Lagrangian submanifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+characteristic+class"} universal characteristic class
            for Lagrangian submanifolds.
          %p
            Specifically, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \hookrightarrow X
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classified
            by a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi LGrass
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i \;\colon\; Y \to LGrass
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %em
              Maslov index of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is the universal Maslov index pulled back along this map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i^\ast u \in H^1(Y,\mathbb{Z})
                  \\,.
          %h2#literature Literature
          %p Original article:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Maslov"}> Victor Maslov
              ,
              %em Théorie des perturbations et méthodes asymptotiques
              (1972)
          %p
            Its cohomological interpretation as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+characteristic+class"} universal characteristic class
            was explained in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Arnold"}> Vladimir Arnold
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Characteristic class entering in quantization conditions
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01075861"} doi
              (В. И. Арнольд, “О характеристическом классе, входящем в условия квантования”, Функц. анализ и его прил., 1:1 (1967), 1–14,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=faa&paperid=2802&what=fullt&option_lang=rus"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrangian_Grassmannian#Maslov_index"} Maslov index
          %p
            A review in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            (Maslov correction) is in
          %ul
            %li
              Sean Bates,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on the geometry of quantization
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.berkeley.edu/~alanw/GofQ.pdf"} pdf
          %p The interpretation of the Maslov index as a quadratic space is due to
          %ul
            %li
              T. Thomas.
              = succeed "." do
                %em The Maslov index as a quadratic space
          %p and this definition and basic examples are briefly collected in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Ranicki"}> Andrew Ranicki
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Maslov Index
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/maslovnotes.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Gérard Lion,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mich%C3%A8le+Vergne"} Michèle Vergne
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Weil representation, Maslov index and Theta series
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 6
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-9154-8"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4684-9154-8
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Maslov gerbe
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 69
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s11005-004-0342-2"} doi
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312274"} arXiv:0312274
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Leray"}> Jean Leray
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lagrangian analysis and quantum mechanics. A mathematical structure related to asymptotic expansions and the Maslov index
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Guillemin"}> Victor Guillemin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Sternberg"} Shlomo Sternberg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric asymptotics
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/online_bks/surv14"} online
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semi-classical analysis
                %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~vwg/semistart.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                J. J. Duistermaat,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Morse index in variational calculus
                %strong 21
                (1976), 2, 173–195,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/duistermaat.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                D. Salamon, E. Zehnder,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Morse theory for periodic solutions of Hamiltonian systems and the Maslov index
                %strong 45
                (1992), no. 10, 1303–1360,
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1002/cpa.3160451004"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joel+Robbin"}> Joel Robbin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dietmar+Salamon"} Dietmar Salamon
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Maslov index for paths
                %strong 32
                (1993), no. 4, 827–844, (
